# Self-Absorption Analysis in LIBS

## 1. Overview

Self-absorption is an important phenomenon in laser-induced plasma spectroscopy that can influence the intensity and shape of observed emission lines.

In LIBS, emitted radiation may be absorbed by cooler atoms of the same species in the plasma, leading to changes in the measured spectral intensity.

## 2. Physical Background

Self-absorption occurs when photons emitted by excited atoms are absorbed by atoms of the same element in lower energy states.

This process can result in:

- Reduced emission line intensity.
- Distortion of spectral line profiles.
- Broadening or flattening of emission peaks.
- Deviations from expected relationships between emission intensity and elemental concentration.

## 3. Relevance to Quantitative LIBS

Accurate quantitative analysis requires careful consideration of self-absorption effects.

If self-absorption is not accounted for, measured emission intensities may not accurately represent the elemental concentration in the investigated material.

## 4. Application to Composite Materials

The MSc research investigated self-absorption effects in laser-induced plasma emission during the spectroscopic analysis of Cu–Al2O3 composite materials.

The objective was to improve the reliability of compositional analysis through consideration of self-absorption effects.

## 5. Research Focus

The investigation focused on:

- The influence of self-absorption on elemental emission lines.
- Changes in measured spectral intensities.
- The implications of self-absorption for quantitative analysis.
- Self-absorption correction in compositional assessment.

